ais523 <ais523@bham.ac.uk> wrote:
> I've been thinking about the subject recently of "cheap ascensions",
> games which are much easier than other games due to extreme amounts of
> luck early. For instance, finding bones with a relevant quest artifact
> and a cloak of MR, and then getting a lamp wish for DSM slightly later
> (I've seen this one happen recently), or early wands of wishing.
> Sometimes, a game is less lucky than that, but nonetheless luckier
> than average; some people consider any early wish as lucky, or any
> remotely useful bones file, or even a coaligned altar in Minetown.
>
> I'm currently interested in writing a (completely optional, of course)
> patch, in order to limit the amount of luck the player can get early.

If you limit luck in the early game I'm not sure if you don't also
limit memorable moments. A lot of player seem to enjoy remembering
being teased by the RNG.

Regardless of that issue wouldn't you also have to limit *bad luck* to
balance it?

> (NAO has already started down this route with its no-bones option,
> allowing players who care to turn off bones loading.) Some obvious
> things to do that can be done automatically are disabling bones, like

I think player disable bones loading mostly for preventing stupid
ascension tricks being invalidated or doubted because of bones
stuffing, be it intentional or not.

Or because some players are afraid of dying in a bones level but that
probably doesn't happen that often.

> NAO does, and disabling all sources of random wish generation (at
> least two variants do something along these lines already, SporkHack
> by messing with generation chances, and UnNetHack by severly limiting
> the power of wishes that don't come from the Castle wand). I suspect

[...] that don't come from the Castle wand *or randomly generated wands
of wishing*.

SporkHack is the variant that disabled random generation of wands of
wishing. UnNetHack has the same probability of random generated WoWs
as Vanilla.

OTOH UnNetHack messes with the "wishability" of DSMs even for WoWs
although I want to change that eventually.

> there are other things that can be done too: some players mention
> early markers, or large armour shops, and of course there are
> startscummers who keep starting until they get early luck (although I
> think the practice is of dubious utility myself except on conduct
> runs, as demonstrated by the fact that startscummers don't generally
> agree on what exactly it is they should startscum for).

I think professional players agree that the starting inventory and
even the starting stats are not at all important in Vanilla if you
don't do a conduct ascension.

> What does rgrn suggest should be prevented from happening, in a game
> where the player (as a self-imposed challenge/conduct, so no need to
> worry about balance concerns unless it completely changes the nature
> of the game) wants to avoid getting lucky early?

Dark mines?

No early coaligned altar?

Guaranteed cross-aligned altar in mine town?

